{
 "id": "asch-conformity",
 "channel": "philosophy",
 "style": "clay",
 "title": "THE CONFORMITY EXPERIMENT",
 "angle": "Would you go along with a crowd that is plainly wrong?",
 "answer": "Sometimes: three in four students went along at least once and about a third of rigged answers followed the crowd, but a single ally cut conformity to about one in twenty, and two-thirds of all answers stayed correct; the crowd bends us more often than it breaks us.",
 "logline": "Three in four students denied their own eyes, so would you go along with a wrong crowd? The others were secret actors, so the subject heard them all first, but the psychologist expected people to stand firm, and a third of rigged answers followed the crowd, but one honest ally cut that to one in twenty, so you need one friend, not a majority, but the retelling skips the biggest number: two-thirds of answers were right, so a crowd bends us but rarely breaks us.",
 "hook_text": "WHICH LINE MATCHES?",
 "hooks": [
  "One honest person in the room cut conformity to one in twenty.",
  "The famous conformity experiment mostly found people telling the truth."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "room"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "In 1951, three in four students denied their own eyes.",
   "say": "In nineteen fifty-one, three in four students denied their own eyes.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "The question was simple: which of three lines matches this one?",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So would you go along with a crowd that's plainly wrong?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "At Swarthmore College, a psychologist sat each student with a group of others.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "Secretly, the others were actors, told to give the same wrong answer.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So the real subject answered near the end, after hearing them all.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But the psychologist expected the opposite: he set out to show that people stand firm.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "Instead, on the rigged rounds, about a third of answers followed the crowd.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Alone, people got it wrong less than once in a hundred.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "But one small change nearly broke the spell.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "When a single actor gave the right answer, conformity fell to about one answer in twenty.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So you don't need a majority to trust your eyes; you need one ally.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But most retellings leave out the biggest number.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "Two-thirds of all answers were right, and a quarter of people never caved once.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So a crowd can bend us, but it rarely breaks us, especially when one person speaks first.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Next time a room is wrong, will you be that one person?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  }
 ],
